var e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"isaiah-scroll","cat":"Manuscripts","label":"Established","head":"A shepherd found a complete Isaiah scroll a thousand years older than any copy we had.","body":"In late 1946 or early 1947, the story goes, a Bedouin shepherd near Qumran threw a rock into a cave and heard pottery break. Inside were the Dead Sea Scrolls. One of them, the Great Isaiah Scroll, is a complete copy of Isaiah from roughly the second century BC. Before that, our oldest full Hebrew copies were from around AD 900–1000. Compared side by side, the text is substantially the same.","say":"The oldest full copy of Isaiah is older than Jesus, and it says substantially the same thing as ours, with many small differences in spelling and wording.","refs":["Isaiah 53:5"],"source":"Great Isaiah Scroll (1QIsa-a), Israel Museum, Jerusalem."},{"id":"pilate-stone","cat":"Archaeology","label":"Established","head":"Pontius Pilate's name was found carved in stone.","body":"For centuries critics noted that Pilate appeared only in the Gospels and a few ancient writers. In 1961 archaeologists at Caesarea Maritima found a limestone block dedicating a building to the emperor Tiberius, signed by Pontius Pilate, prefect of Judea. It matches the title and the time.","say":"The man who sentenced Jesus signed a building. We have the stone.","refs":["Matthew 27:2"],"source":"Pilate Stone, discovered 1961; Israel Museum, Jerusalem."},{"id":"caiaphas","cat":"Archaeology","label":"Established","head":"A bone box most scholars link to the high priest who tried Jesus was found by construction workers.","body":"In 1990 workers building a park in Jerusalem broke into a burial cave. One ornate ossuary, a box for bones, was inscribed \\"Joseph son of Caiaphas.\\" Most scholars identify him with the high priest Caiaphas who presided at Jesus' trial. Josephus gives his full name as Joseph Caiaphas.","say":"They very likely found Caiaphas's bone box by accident, during construction work in Jerusalem.","refs":["Matthew 26:57"],"source":"Caiaphas ossuary, discovered 1990; Israel Museum, Jerusalem."},{"id":"tel-dan","cat":"Archaeology","label":"Established","head":"\\"The House of David\\" shows up on an enemy's victory monument.","body":"Scholars once argued King David was a legend. In 1993 a stone was found at Tel Dan in northern Israel, carved in the 800s BC by an Aramean king boasting about defeating the king of Israel and the \\"House of David.\\" It is the earliest widely accepted mention of David's royal line outside the Bible, written by an enemy of that dynasty.","say":"The oldest clear mention of David's dynasty outside the Bible comes from that dynasty's enemy.","refs":["2 Samuel 7:16"],"source":"Tel Dan Stele, discovered 1993–94; Israel Museum, Jerusalem."},{"id":"siloam","cat":"Archaeology","label":"Established","head":"The pool where Jesus healed a blind man turned up during a sewer repair.","body":"John 9 says Jesus sent a man born blind to wash in the Pool of Siloam. In 2004, workers repairing a sewer pipe in Jerusalem uncovered steps of a large pool from the time of Jesus. Coins in the plaster and the debris show it was built in the century before Jesus and used until AD 70.","say":"The Pool of Siloam was found in 2004, by guys fixing a sewer.","refs":["John 9:7"],"source":"Pool of Siloam excavations, 2004 onward, City of David, Jerusalem."},{"id":"bethesda","cat":"Archaeology","label":"Established","head":"John said the pool had five porches. Critics said that was invented. It wasn't.","body":"John 5 describes the Pool of Bethesda with five colonnades, an odd number for a pool, and long treated as a sign John didn't know Jerusalem. Excavations near the Church of St. Anne uncovered two pools separated by a central wall: a layout that fits four porches around the edge plus one across the middle. Five.","say":"The five-porch pool in John that critics doubted turned out to be real: twin pools with a layout that fits five porches.","refs":["John 5:2"],"source":"Pool of Bethesda excavations, St. Anne's, Jerusalem (19th–20th century)."},{"id":"tacitus","cat":"Jesus in history","label":"Established","head":"A Roman historian who despised Christians confirms Jesus was executed under Pilate.","body":"Writing around AD 116, Tacitus, no friend of the faith, explains that the name Christian comes from \\"Christus,\\" who \\"suffered the extreme penalty during the reign of Tiberius at the hands of Pontius Pilate.\\" Josephus, a Jewish historian, separately mentions \\"James, the brother of Jesus who was called Christ.\\" Almost no historian today, Christian or not, doubts Jesus lived and was crucified.","say":"Even a Roman historian who despised Christians wrote down that Pilate executed Jesus.","refs":["Luke 23:24"],"source":"Tacitus, Annals 15.44; Josephus, Antiquities 20.9.1."},{"id":"yehohanan","cat":"Archaeology","label":"Established","head":"We have a crucified man's heel bone with the nail still in it.","body":"Some argued crucified bodies were never given a proper burial, which would make the Gospels' tomb story unlikely. In 1968, in a Jerusalem tomb, archaeologists found the ossuary of a man named Yehohanan from the first century. His heel bone still has an iron nail through it. A crucified Jew, from Jesus' time, buried in a family tomb.","say":"There's a 2,000-year-old heel bone with a crucifixion nail still in it, and he got a proper tomb.","refs":["John 19:41–42"],"source":"Giv'at ha-Mivtar tomb, 1968; Israel Museum, Jerusalem."},{"id":"silver-scrolls","cat":"Manuscripts","label":"Established","head":"The oldest Bible verse ever found was worn as a necklace.","body":"In 1979, two tiny rolled silver amulets were found in a burial cave at Ketef Hinnom in Jerusalem. Unrolled, they carry the priestly blessing from Numbers 6: \\"The LORD bless you and keep you.\\" They date to around 600 BC, before the Babylonian exile, and are the oldest surviving text of any Bible passage.","say":"The oldest Bible verse we have was a blessing someone wore around their neck 2,600 years ago.","refs":["Numbers 6:24–26"],"source":"Ketef Hinnom amulets, discovered 1979; Israel Museum, Jerusalem."},{"id":"hezekiah","cat":"Archaeology","label":"Established","head":"You can still walk through King Hezekiah's tunnel.","body":"2 Kings 20 and 2 Chronicles 32 say Hezekiah brought water into Jerusalem by a conduit as Assyria threatened. The tunnel is real (about 530 meters cut through rock) and an inscription found in it in 1880 describes the moment the two digging crews, working from opposite ends, heard each other and broke through.","say":"Hezekiah's tunnel is still there. People wade through it with flashlights.","refs":["2 Kings 20:20"],"source":"Siloam Inscription, 1880; Istanbul Archaeology Museums."},{"id":"micah","cat":"Prophecy","label":"Established","head":"Micah named Bethlehem as the Messiah's birthplace about 700 years early.","body":"Micah wrote in the 700s BC that a ruler would come out of Bethlehem, a small town in Judah. When the wise men asked where the Messiah would be born, Herod's own scholars quoted Micah and pointed to Bethlehem. Some scholars date this passage later than Micah himself, but all agree it is centuries older than Jesus; what you make of the fulfillment is the question.","say":"The birthplace was in print seven centuries ahead of time.","refs":["Micah 5:2","Matthew 2:4–6"],"source":"Micah 5:2; Matthew 2:4–6."},{"id":"isaiah53","cat":"Prophecy","label":"Established","head":"Isaiah 53 describes a man \\"pierced for our transgressions\\", and we have a copy older than Jesus.","body":"Isaiah 53 describes a servant who is rejected, wounded for others' sins, silent before his accusers, and buried with the rich. Because the Great Isaiah Scroll predates Jesus, nobody can argue Christians wrote it after the fact. Fair note: most Jewish readers understand the servant as Israel. Christians, starting with the apostles, read it as Jesus.","say":"Read Isaiah 53 to someone and ask who it's about. Then tell them it's older than Jesus.","refs":["Isaiah 53:5","Acts 8:32–35"],"source":"Isaiah 53; Great Isaiah Scroll (1QIsa-a)."},{"id":"psalm22","cat":"Prophecy","label":"Debated","head":"Psalm 22: \\"They pierce my hands and my feet\\", or \\"like a lion\\"?","body":"Psalm 22 opens with the words Jesus cried on the cross and describes mocking crowds and gambling for clothes. Verse 16 is the fight: most Christian translations read \\"they pierced my hands and my feet,\\" following the Greek Septuagint and a Dead Sea fragment; the standard Hebrew text reads \\"like a lion, my hands and my feet.\\" Both sides have real manuscript arguments. Use this one carefully.","say":"Psalm 22 lines up with the cross in a dozen ways. One word in it is genuinely argued over.","refs":["Psalm 22:16","Matthew 27:46"],"source":"Psalm 22; Septuagint; Nahal Hever scroll 5/6HevPs."},{"id":"shroud","cat":"Artifacts","label":"Debated","head":"The Shroud of Turin: the most studied cloth in the world, and still unsettled.","body":"A linen cloth bearing the faint image of a crucified man, kept in Turin since 1578. In 1988 three labs carbon-dated a sample to AD 1260–1390. Defenders argue the sample came from a medieval repair patch; critics say the dating stands. Nobody has convincingly reproduced how the image was formed. Your faith does not rest on it; the empty tomb does.","say":"Carbon dating says medieval, and how the image was made is still argued over. Most scientists accept the date; defenders still contest it.","refs":["John 20:6–7"],"source":"Damon et al., Nature 337 (1989); STURP report (1981)."},{"id":"james-ossuary","cat":"Artifacts","label":"Debated","head":"A bone box reading \\"James, son of Joseph, brother of Jesus.\\"","body":"Announced in 2002, the ossuary itself is genuinely ancient. The question is whether the last phrase, \\"brother of Jesus\\", was added by a forger. Israel's antiquities authority said yes; after a years-long trial, the collector was acquitted of forgery in 2012, but the court did not rule the inscription authentic. Scholars remain split.","say":"There's a box that might belong to Jesus' brother. A court couldn't prove it was faked.","refs":["Galatians 1:19"],"source":"Jerusalem District Court verdict, March 2012."},{"id":"manuscripts","cat":"Manuscripts","label":"Established","head":"No other ancient book comes close in surviving copies.","body":"There are over 5,800 known Greek manuscripts of the New Testament, some within a century or two of the originals. By comparison, the works of most famous ancient writers survive in a handful to a few hundred copies, often a thousand years after they wrote. Copies differ, mostly in small ways, and scholars track every variant, which is how the original wording can be recovered with high confidence, apart from a few disputed passages.","say":"The New Testament survives in more handwritten copies than any other book from the ancient world, though most were copied in the Middle Ages.","refs":["1 Peter 1:25"],"source":"Institute for New Testament Textual Research (Münster) manuscript list."},{"id":"verses","cat":"Fun facts","label":"Established","head":"Chapter and verse numbers aren't in the original Bible.","body":"The biblical authors wrote without chapters or verses. Stephen Langton, later Archbishop of Canterbury, added the chapter divisions in the early 1200s. The printer Robert Estienne added New Testament verse numbers in 1551, legend says partly while riding on horseback. Useful, but sometimes they split a thought in the middle.","say":"Verse numbers were added in 1551. Sometimes they chop a sentence in half.","refs":["John 3:16"],"source":"Langton chapters c. 1227; Estienne Greek New Testament, 1551."},{"id":"aramaic","cat":"Jesus in history","label":"Established","head":"The Gospels kept some of Jesus' exact Aramaic words.","body":"The New Testament is written in Greek, but Jesus' everyday language was Aramaic, and a few of his actual words were kept untranslated: \\"Talitha koum\\" (\\"little girl, get up\\"), \\"Ephphatha\\" (\\"be opened\\"), and from the cross, \\"Eloi, Eloi, lema sabachthani.\\" These are the closest we get to hearing his voice.","say":"A few of Jesus' exact words survive in his own language; 'Talitha koum' is one.","refs":["Mark 5:41","Mark 15:34"],"source":"Mark 5:41; 7:34; 15:34."},{"id":"yeshua","cat":"Jesus in history","label":"Established","head":"Jesus and Joshua are the same name.","body":"Jesus' Hebrew name was Yeshua, a short form of Yehoshua (Joshua), meaning \\"the LORD saves.\\" It was one of the most common Jewish names of his day. \\"Jesus\\" comes to English through Greek (Iesous) and Latin. The angel's instruction makes the meaning the point: name him this, because he will save his people.","say":"Jesus' name literally means 'God saves'. Same name as Joshua.","refs":["Matthew 1:21"],"source":"Matthew 1:21; standard Hebrew lexicons."},{"id":"hittites","cat":"Archaeology","label":"Established","head":"The Hittites were a near-forgotten footnote outside the Bible, until their capital was dug up.","body":"The Bible mentions the Hittites dozens of times, but outside it they were nearly unknown, and some 19th-century scholars doubted they mattered or even existed as described. Excavations at Hattusa in Turkey from 1906 uncovered their capital and thousands of clay tablets. They had been a major empire.","say":"For centuries almost nothing was known of the Hittites outside the Bible. Then we found their capital and their library.","refs":["Genesis 23:10"],"source":"Hattusa (Boğazköy) excavations, from 1906; UNESCO World Heritage Site."}]`),t=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"ep38","title":"What Happens the Moment You Die","category":"death","disputed":false,"crisis":false,"short":["Your body dies. You do not.","There is no waiting room.","It is better, but it is not the finish.","Your body is coming back.","Then judgment, and the verdict is already in."],"intro":["You are going to die. So is everyone you love. That sentence is not meant to frighten you, and the Bible does not treat it as the worst thing that can happen to a person. It treats it as a door. And it is surprisingly specific about what is on the other side of it.","Here is the problem. Almost everything you picture when you think about dying came from somewhere other than scripture. Clouds. Harps. A waiting room. Floating around as a spirit watching your own funeral. Becoming an angel. A long sleep until the end of the world.","Some of that is films. Some of it is things people said kindly at funerals because they did not know what else to say. And it matters, because Hebrews says people are destined to die once, and after that to face judgment, and a person who is frightened of the wrong picture will spend their last months dreading a God who came to get them.","So here are five things the Bible actually says happen, in the order it says they happen. This is for you, and it is for you to send to someone who has just been given a diagnosis and is lying awake with it."],"steps":[{"title":"Your body dies. You do not.","ref":"Ecclesiastes 12:7","body":["Two things go in two directions. The body goes back to the ground. The Bible is unsentimental about that, and so is every graveside. But the person is not the body alone. The spirit returns to God who gave it.","Notice who it returns to. Not to the universe, not to a vague light, not back around into another body. To God. The one who gave it in the first place.","This is the piece that changes how dying feels. You are not being switched off. The part of you that has been you since you were a child does not stop when your heart does. It goes somewhere, to Someone, and He is expecting it."],"thisWeek":"if you are afraid of dying, say that out loud to God once, in those words. Not a formal prayer. He already knows the number of your days, and He is not startled by the fear.","disagree":null},{"title":"There is no waiting room.","ref":"Luke 23:43","body":["A convicted criminal is dying next to Jesus. He has no baptism, no church membership, no time left to fix his life. He has one sentence: remember me.","And Jesus does not say *eventually*. He does not say *after a long sleep*, or *once you have been processed*. He says **today**.","That word does a lot of work. Whatever else is true about the end of the world, the Bible's picture of the moment you die is not a queue. For the person who belongs to Christ it is immediate, and it is not a place. It is a person. *With me.*"],"thisWeek":"read Luke 23 verses 32 to 43 slowly, once. It takes four minutes. Watch how little the man had to offer.","disagree":null},{"title":"It is better. But it is not the finish.","ref":"Philippians 1:23","body":["Paul is writing from prison and genuinely cannot decide which he would rather have: staying alive to help people, or dying. He calls being with Christ better by far.","So the in-between is good. It is conscious. He is looking forward to it.","But, and most people miss this, Paul does not call it the end of the story. The New Testament treats the time between your death and the resurrection as real and happy and *temporary*. Something better is still coming. Which leads to the thing almost nobody expects."],"thisWeek":"notice how you talk about someone who has died. If every sentence stops at \\"they're in a better place,\\" you have stopped halfway through what the Bible promises.","disagree":null},{"title":"Your body is coming back.","ref":"1 Corinthians 15:42–44","body":["The Christian hope has never been that you escape your body. It is that you get it back, fixed.","Same seed, different plant. That is Paul's picture: the thing that goes into the ground and the thing that comes up are continuous, but what comes up is not weak and not dying. Jesus did not rise as a ghost. He cooked breakfast on a beach for His friends.","Whatever is wrong with your body right now (the thing you take tablets for, the thing that aches when it rains, the thing the scan found) is not a permanent feature of you."],"thisWeek":"name one specific thing about your body you assume you are stuck with. That one is on the list of things that gets raised imperishable.","disagree":null},{"title":"Then judgment. And if you are in Christ, the verdict is already in.","ref":"Romans 8:1","body":["Judgment is real. The Bible does not soften it and neither will I. Everyone faces it.","But read that verse again, and watch the word *now*. Paul does not say the condemnation is postponed, or that it will probably go well on the day. He says for the person in Christ there is **now** none. The verdict was reached at the cross and it is already in the file.","That is the difference between facing judgment and dreading it. You are not walking in to find out. You are walking in to hear read aloud what was settled a long time ago."],"thisWeek":"if you are not sure you are in Christ, that is the only question on this list worth losing sleep over, and it is answered the way the dying man answered it. He asked.","disagree":null}]},{"id":"ep39","title":"Is Suicide the Unforgivable Sin?","category":"death","disputed":false,"crisis":true,"short":["The unforgivable sin has a name. This isn't it.","Death cannot separate you. Paul put it first on the list.","You are not saved by your last act, so you are not unsaved by it.","God is closest to the crushed.","When Elijah wanted to die, God sent food and sleep."],"intro":["You are probably here for one of two reasons. Either someone you love died this way and somebody told you where they are now. Or it is about you, and you want to know what God thinks of you for it.","Either way, you have been told something that is not in the Bible. I want to show you the actual text, because the actual text is kinder than the rumour.","For centuries people have been taught that suicide sends a person straight to hell. It has been said at gravesides. It has been used to refuse funerals. And the damage is specific: a mother is told she will never see her son again, and she believes it, because a man in a collar said it.","Here is what matters: **that teaching is not in scripture.** It was built later, mostly out of one argument that it is a murder you cannot repent of afterwards. We will deal with that argument directly.","**And before anything else, if you are thinking about ending your life, call or text 988 right now if you are in the US or Canada. Someone answers, tonight, and they will not be shocked by anything you say.** The rest of this lesson will still be here afterwards.","Five things the Bible actually says. Some of this is disputed between churches and I will tell you where, plainly, rather than pretend it is settled."],"steps":[{"title":"The unforgivable sin has a name, and this isn't it.","ref":"Mark 3:28–29","body":["Jesus names it. He is specific. One thing is called unforgivable, and it is blasphemy against the Holy Spirit, spoken by men who were watching Him heal people and calling it the work of the devil. A settled, knowing, lifelong refusal to call good good.","Look at the first half of that sentence, because people skip it: *the sons of men will be forgiven all sins and blasphemies.* All. That is Jesus setting the default, and then naming the single exception.","Suicide is not the exception. It is not mentioned. If it were the one unforgivable act, this was the verse to say so in, and He said something else."],"thisWeek":"if you have been afraid you committed the unforgivable sin, the fact that you are afraid of it is itself the evidence you have not. Nobody who has settled into hating God lies awake worrying about it.","disagree":null},{"title":"Death is on the list of things that cannot separate you.","ref":"Romans 8:38–39","body":["Paul is building the longest list he can think of, and he puts **death** first.","He does not write \\"neither death, unless it was self-inflicted.\\" He does not leave a gap. The whole force of the sentence is that he cannot find a thing in creation big enough to break the hold, and he is clearly trying.","The Bible records several people who took their own lives: Saul, Ahithophel, Judas. It tells you what they did. In not one case does it then tell you they are therefore beyond God's reach. The silence is worth noticing, because scripture is not usually shy."],"thisWeek":"read Romans 8 from verse 31 to the end. Six verses. Notice it is written to frightened people, not confident ones.","disagree":null},{"title":"You are not saved by your last act.","ref":"Ephesians 2:8–9","body":["Here is the old argument, stated fairly: suicide is a sin, and you cannot repent of it afterwards, so you die with unrepented sin.","The problem is what that assumes: that salvation is a balance you are keeping level, and whatever is in your hand at the final second decides it. But Paul says it is a gift, not a wage. Not from yourselves. Not by works.","If your standing with God depended on dying in a tidy state of mind, then every Christian killed instantly in a car crash mid-argument would be lost too. Nobody actually believes that. The same logic applies here, and people only suspend it for this one death because of how much it frightens them."],"thisWeek":"if someone told you your person is in hell, you are allowed to ask them which verse says so. There isn't one.","disagree":"Catholic teaching historically treated suicide as a mortal sin, and some still describe it that way. But the modern Catechism itself says grave psychological suffering can diminish a person's responsibility, and that we should not despair of their salvation. Most Protestant traditions hold that a believer's salvation is not undone by it. You will find people who disagree. You will not find a verse that settles it against the grieving."},{"title":"God is nearest to the crushed, not furthest.","ref":"Psalm 34:18","body":["Not tolerant of. Not disappointed in. **Close to.**","This is the piece that gets lost when the conversation turns into a courtroom argument about guilt. The Bible's consistent posture toward a person in unbearable pain is to move toward them.","A mind in that much pain is not making a free, clear-eyed choice to spit at God. It is an organ in agony that has stopped being able to see any other door. Scripture treats that as something to be rescued from, not prosecuted."],"thisWeek":"if this is about you, tell one actual human being this week. Not a post. A person, out loud. That is the whole assignment.","disagree":null},{"title":"When Elijah asked to die, God sent food and sleep.","ref":"1 Kings 19:4–5","body":["This is one of the great prophets of Israel, days after the biggest victory of his life, sitting under a bush asking God to kill him.","Watch what God does not do. No lecture. No rebuke. No \\"how dare you, after everything I have done.\\" God lets him sleep. Then feeds him. Then lets him sleep again. Then feeds him again. Only much later, and very gently, does He ask him a question.","Food. Rest. A quiet voice. That is God's documented response to a man who wanted to die, and it is strikingly close to what a good doctor would do."],"thisWeek":"if you are at the bush, start where God started Elijah. Eat something. Sleep. Tell one person. In that order. The large questions can wait until you have done those three.","disagree":null}]},{"id":"ep40","title":"What Strong Christians Do Every Day","category":"daily","disputed":false,"crisis":false,"short":["One chapter, same time, same chair.","The same three names, by name.","Deal with it the same day.","One thank-you, out loud.","One person who would notice you were gone."],"intro":["You do not need a personality transplant to walk with God daily. You need about fifteen minutes and five things you actually repeat. The people you think of as spiritually solid are not running on willpower you do not have. They are running on a handful of small things they stopped renegotiating.","Here is what kills it, and it is almost never a loss of faith.","It is the restart cycle. You go hard for nine days. You miss a day. You miss two. And then the quiet verdict arrives: *I'm clearly not disciplined enough for this*, and you shelve the whole thing until a Monday, or a New Year, or a hard week that scares you back.","The enemy of a daily walk is not atheism. It is all-or-nothing. Nothing in scripture asks you to be impressive. It asks you to be *near*, repeatedly, and the all-or-nothing instinct is what keeps breaking the repetition.","So here are five daily habits, each one small enough to survive a bad week. This is for you if you keep starting over, or if you know someone who has been meaning to get back into it for about a year."],"steps":[{"title":"Read a small fixed amount, same time, same place.","ref":"Psalm 119:105","body":["A lamp for your feet is not a floodlight over the whole valley. It shows you the next step. That is the actual promise, and it is a daily-sized one.","Which is why the plan matters less than the repetition. People fail Bible reading by starting in Genesis in January with a plan built for a scholar, hitting Leviticus in February, and quietly stopping. Then the shame of the bookmark keeps them away for a year.","Make it absurdly small and absurdly fixed. One chapter. Same time. Same chair. The fixed chair is doing more work than you think: you are not relying on remembering, you are relying on the room."],"thisWeek":"pick one chapter a day, one time, one place. Start in Mark, not Genesis. It is the shortest gospel and it moves. If you miss a day, read the next chapter, not the one you missed. Never go back and catch up; that is the rule that keeps people reading in March.","disagree":null},{"title":"Pray for the same few people, by name.","ref":"Philippians 4:6","body":["Most people's prayer life dies of vagueness. \\"Bless everyone, help me be better, amen.\\" You cannot be faithful in something that has no shape, and you cannot notice an answer to a request you never actually made.","Notice what Paul says: *present your requests.* Specific things, handed over.","So name them. The same three or four people, every morning, out loud or on paper. Your mother. The friend who is drinking again. The person at work you find difficult. Say what you are actually asking for."],"thisWeek":"write three names on a card and keep it where you drink your coffee. Pray those same three for seven days. On day seven, look back and see what moved. This is the habit where you start collecting evidence.","disagree":null},{"title":"Deal with it the same day.","ref":"1 John 1:9","body":["Guilt does not sit still. Left alone overnight it hardens into distance, and distance is what actually stops people praying: not the sin itself, but the awkwardness of coming back after avoiding it for a fortnight.","So close the gap daily, in about thirty seconds. Name the thing plainly. Not \\"sorry for everything.\\" The actual thing.","Look at the two words John chose: *faithful and just.* Not reluctant. Forgiving you is not a mood God has to be talked into. It is something He has bound Himself to do."],"thisWeek":"before you sleep, name one specific thing from that day and leave it there. No rehearsing it, no extended self-criticism. That is not repentance, it is just anxiety wearing repentance's coat.","disagree":null},{"title":"Say one thank-you out loud.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 5:18","body":["Read that carefully, because it is widely misquoted. It does not say give thanks *for* all circumstances. You are not required to be grateful your father is ill. It says *in*: thank Him inside the circumstance, for whatever is genuinely good while the hard thing is still happening.","This is the habit that changes your mood fastest, and it is the one people skip because it sounds soft. It is not soft. A mind left on its own drifts toward threat; naming one good thing out loud is a daily correction to that drift.","Out loud matters. Thinking something vaguely grateful is not the same act."],"thisWeek":"one sentence, spoken, every day. \\"Thank you for ___.\\" The smaller and more specific, the better: the coffee, the lift arriving, the fact your son rang.","disagree":null},{"title":"Be around other Christians on purpose.","ref":"Hebrews 10:24–25","body":["This is the daily habit that is not daily, and it is the one that holds the other four up.","The writer calls dropping out a *habit*. Notice that. Nobody decides to stop meeting. It erodes. A busy fortnight, an awkward Sunday, a season where it feels like effort, and six months later you are a Christian who has not said a Christian sentence out loud to another person since spring.","The four habits above are all things you do alone, and alone is exactly where they quietly die. You need at least one person who would notice."],"thisWeek":"text one Christian you know and ask them a real question about their week. Not a group. One person. That is how it starts every time.","disagree":null}]},{"id":"ep41","title":"Can You Lose Your Salvation?","category":"salvation","disputed":true,"crisis":false,"short":["No one can snatch you out. His grip, not yours.","The warnings are real. Do not let anyone skip them.","Both camps tell you to do the same thing.","Nobody falls out by accident, and the father runs.","The fear itself is evidence the Spirit is still at work."],"intro":["If you are asking this question, I can usually guess why. It is not a theological curiosity. It is because of something you did, or something you keep doing, and you want to know whether you are still in.","So I am going to show you both sets of verses: the ones that promise you are held, and the ones that warn you can fall. Including the ones your side usually skips.","There are two bad answers on offer here, and both of them wreck people.","The first says it is impossible to fall away, full stop, which means the Bible's warnings are aimed at nobody, and a man can live however he likes on the strength of a prayer he said at fifteen.","The second leaves you checking your own pulse every night. Saved on Tuesday, lost on Thursday, re-saved on Sunday. People living that way are not holy. They are exhausted, and they eventually stop trying.","The honest position is harder than either, and it is where the text actually leaves you.","Five things. Two of them will sound like they contradict each other, and I am going to leave that tension standing rather than flatten it, because serious Christians have disagreed about this for four hundred years and I am not settling it in one lesson."],"steps":[{"title":"The verses that say you are held.","ref":"John 10:28–29","body":["Read what is actually promised. Not that you will feel secure. Not that you will never fail. That no one can snatch you out.","And notice the grip is doubled: His hand, and the Father's. Jesus is stacking the image on purpose, for people who were frightened.","This is the bedrock for the whole tradition that says a true believer cannot finally be lost. It is not a wishful reading. It is the plain sense of a very strong sentence."],"thisWeek":"if your faith currently rests on how well you are doing, notice that this verse does not mention your grip at all. It is entirely about His.","disagree":null},{"title":"The verses that warn you can fall.","ref":"Hebrews 6:4–6","body":["I am not going to pretend that verse is not there, and I am not going to rush past it to get back to the comfortable one.","Whoever is being described has been enlightened, has tasted the heavenly gift, has shared in the Holy Spirit. That is not a description of an outsider. And the writer says of such a person that falling away is possible and serious.","Add Galatians 5:4, where Paul tells people they have *fallen away from grace*. These are not obscure corners. They are warnings written to churches.","**Where the two camps split, fairly stated.** One side reads Hebrews 6 as describing people who were never genuinely converted: close to the fire, warmed by it, never in it. The other reads it plainly: real believers, real danger. Both are reading the same page in good faith. Pretending otherwise is how you end up trusting a teacher who is managing your feelings instead of teaching you."],"thisWeek":"read Hebrews 6:1–12 whole. Verse 9 matters: right after the warning, the writer says he is *convinced of better things* in their case. The warning and the confidence sit in the same paragraph.","disagree":null},{"title":"Both sides tell you to do the same thing.","ref":"2 Peter 1:10","body":["Here is what nobody tells you about this argument: the two camps arrive at identical instructions.","If you believe you cannot lose it, Peter still says make every effort to confirm it. If you believe you can, Peter says the same. Nobody's conclusion is \\"so relax.\\"","That is why this dispute, fierce as it is in comment sections, changes almost nothing about your Monday. Both sides say: keep going, take sin seriously, stay close. A doctrine of security that produces carelessness is not what either camp teaches."],"thisWeek":"stop trying to settle the argument and do the thing both sides agree on. One concrete step back toward God this week, chosen by you.","disagree":null},{"title":"Nobody falls out by accident.","ref":"Luke 15:20","body":["This is the part that answers what you are actually afraid of.","Read the warning passages carefully and you find they describe something deliberate and settled: a person who has seen clearly and turned away and stayed turned. Hebrews is not describing someone who had a terrible month, or lost their temper, or relapsed, or stopped feeling anything in worship.","The son in this story did everything wrong, on purpose, for a long time. And the father is not waiting at the door with conditions. He is running, which, for a dignified man in that culture, was humiliating, and that is the point.","Nobody who is frightened of having fallen away has fallen away in the sense Hebrews means. The fear is the opposite of the settled hardness being described."],"thisWeek":"if there is a distance, close it today rather than waiting until you feel worthy of closing it. The father ran while the son still smelled of pigs.","disagree":null},{"title":"The fear itself is the evidence.","ref":"Romans 8:15–16","body":["Paul names the exact state most people are in when they type this question into a search bar: *a spirit of slavery that returns you to fear*, and he says that is not what the Spirit produces.","Think about who worries about this. Not the person who has walked away and is content. That person is not searching this at midnight. The ones who agonise are the ones who still want Him, and wanting Him is itself the work of the Spirit in you.","That is not a trick of logic. It is what Paul says is happening: the Spirit testifying alongside your spirit, often underneath feelings that are telling you the opposite."],"thisWeek":"when the fear comes, answer it with the one word Paul gives you. Say *Father*, out loud. The fact that the word is available to you is the testimony he is describing.","disagree":null}]},{"id":"ep42","title":"Is Hell Forever?","category":"death","disputed":true,"crisis":false,"short":["The whole argument turns on one word in Matthew 25:46.","View one: endless conscious torment. Majority view. Strongest text: Revelation 20:10.","View two: destruction. Strongest text: the Bible's own word, *perish*.","View three: all reconciled. Smallest. Weakest against Jesus' own warnings.","All three agree: real, permanent, and the way out is open today."],"intro":["Nobody asks this one casually. People ask it because someone they love died without faith, and they are trying to work out what they are supposed to believe is happening right now.","So here is the honest version. Not the comfortable one, not the frightening one. What the text says, where the words are genuinely uncertain, and what every serious position agrees on.","The danger here is a confident teacher.","You can find a man who will tell you, with total certainty and no hesitation, that the answer is endless conscious torment. You can find another who will tell you with equal certainty that the lost simply cease to exist. Both will sound authoritative. Both are reading the same Bible, in the same language, and have been disagreeing for about eighteen hundred years.","What should worry you is not the disagreement. It is being handed one answer with the disagreement hidden, because then you build your picture of God's character on something you were never told was contested.","So: one verse everybody quotes, then the three positions Bible-believing Christians actually hold, each given its best case, then the thing all three agree on. I will tell you where the evidence genuinely runs out."],"steps":[{"title":"The verse everyone quotes, and the balance inside it.","ref":"Matthew 25:46","body":["This is the sentence the whole argument turns on, and you can see why. The same word, *eternal*, is applied to both destinations in the same breath. That is the strongest argument for the traditional view: whatever *eternal* means for life, it means for punishment. You cannot stretch one and shrink the other.","But notice the other word, because it is doing real work. It says eternal **punishment**, not eternal *punishing*. A punishment can be permanent without the process being ongoing: a life sentence and an execution are both permanent, and they are not the same event.","That is not a dodge. It is the actual hinge the three views turn on, and it is sitting in the verse everybody quotes at each other."],"thisWeek":"read Matthew 25:31–46 whole. Notice who is being separated and on what basis. It is not what most people assume.","disagree":null},{"title":"View one: it is endless conscious torment.","ref":"Revelation 20:10","body":["This is the historic majority position across most of church history, and its case is strong.","*Tormented day and night for ever and ever* is about as explicit as language gets. Add Matthew 25:46's parallel, and Mark 9:48's worm that does not die and fire that is not quenched, and you have a consistent picture.","**The honest weak point:** Revelation 20:10 names the devil, the beast and the false prophet. Whether that same sentence extends to every lost human being is read into it, reasonably, but it is read in rather than stated. Revelation is also the most image-dense book in the Bible, and people who take its beasts and bowls as symbols sometimes take its duration literally without explaining why that one detail switches modes."],"thisWeek":"if this is the view you hold, make sure you can say what its weakest point is. A faith that cannot survive knowing the counter-argument is not strong, it is just unexamined.","disagree":null},{"title":"View two: the lost are destroyed, not endlessly tortured.","ref":"Matthew 10:28","body":["This is called conditional immortality, or annihilationism, and it is held by a serious minority of Bible-believing Christians, including, tentatively, John Stott.","Its case is the Bible's own vocabulary. *Destroy.* *Perish*, as in John 3:16, \\"shall not perish but have eternal life.\\" *Death*, *consumed*, *burned up*, *ashes under the soles of your feet* in Malachi 4. The repeated language of the end of the wicked is the language of ending, not of ongoing existence.","The strongest single piece of evidence is Jude 7, which says Sodom and Gomorrah suffered *the punishment of eternal fire*. That fire is not still burning. What was eternal about it was the result, not the duration, which is precisely the distinction Step 1 opened up.","**The honest weak point:** it has to account for Revelation 20:10's *for ever and ever*, and the explanations offered are reasonable but less immediately natural than the plain reading."],"thisWeek":"look up John 3:16 and read the word *perish* as though you had never heard the verse before.","disagree":null},{"title":"View three: all are eventually reconciled.","ref":"1 Timothy 2:3–4","body":["This is the smallest of the three and the one furthest from the historic mainstream, but it has been held by real Christians since at least Origen in the third century, and you should know it exists rather than meet it cold in a comment thread.","Its case rests on verses about God's desire that all be saved, and on passages like Colossians 1:20 about reconciling all things to Himself.","**The honest weak point, and it is the heaviest of the three:** it has to explain why Jesus warned about this subject as urgently and as often as He did. If everyone arrives eventually, the warnings in Matthew 25 are strangely worded. Most Christians across most of history have found that objection decisive, and I think it is the strongest argument against the view."],"thisWeek":"notice that I have just told you the weakness of a view I am presenting. Expect that from anyone teaching you about this.","disagree":null},{"title":"What all three agree on.","ref":"2 Peter 3:9","body":["Here is where the three positions converge, and it is more than people expect.","All three say hell is real. All three say it is terrible. The first two say it is permanent; the third says it ends. All three say Jesus is the way out and that the invitation is open right now. And all three stand under this verse: that God is not eager for anyone to end up there, and that the delay you are living in is itself patience.","Which means the practical answer to \\"is hell forever\\" is the same in all three systems: it is not a thing to gamble on, and the door is open today. That is not me ducking the question. It is the actual overlap.","**On the person you are really asking about:** you do not know what passed between them and God in the last hours, and neither does anyone who tells you confidently that you do. The thief on the cross had minutes. Leave it where you genuinely cannot see, and refuse to let anyone fill that space for you with a certainty they do not have."],"thisWeek":"if you have been carrying a verdict somebody else pronounced over a person you loved, put it down. They were not in a position to give it.","disagree":null}]},{"id":"ep43","title":"Do Catholics Go to Heaven?","category":"churches","disputed":true,"crisis":false,"short":["The question is what you trust, not what you are called.","Both confess the same creeds about Christ.","The real disagreement is justification, and it is genuine.","Nobody gets in on membership. That warning is for everyone.","The verdict is Christ's. Go and ask your family the real question."],"intro":["You are not asking this in the abstract. You are asking because of a person. A grandmother with a rosary. A husband who crosses himself. Somebody told you they are not really saved, and you have been carrying it since.","So let us do this properly: what Catholics actually teach, where the real disagreement is, and what the Bible says the question turns on.","Here is the danger, and it runs both directions.","There are Protestants who will tell you, flatly, that no Catholic is saved. There are Catholics who will tell you the Reformation put everyone else outside the Church. Both speak with total confidence. Both are describing the other side's beliefs from memory rather than from their actual documents.","And the cost is paid at kitchen tables. People stop praying with their own parents. A son decides his dying father is lost and says nothing to him at the end, because what would be the point.","Getting this wrong is not an academic mistake. So let us be careful.","Five things. I will tell you plainly where Catholics and Protestants genuinely disagree (there are real differences and I am not going to paper over them) and what the New Testament says the question actually rests on."],"steps":[{"title":"The question is answered by what saves, not by a label.","ref":"Romans 10:9","body":["Notice what Paul does not say. He does not name a denomination. He does not list a parish or a tradition. He gives two things: a confession and a belief.","This is the measure the New Testament uses, and it is the one to hold onto while everything else gets complicated. The question is never \\"which building\\"; it is whether a person is trusting Christ.","That cuts both ways, and uncomfortably. It means a Catholic trusting Christ is saved. It also means a Protestant who has never trusted anything but his own church attendance is not, and he will be very surprised."],"thisWeek":"if you have been assessing someone by their denomination, ask the question Paul actually asks instead. You may have to actually talk to them to find out.","disagree":null},{"title":"What both sides confess together.","ref":"Ephesians 4:4–6","body":["Most people have no idea how much is shared, because nobody lists it.","Catholics and Protestants both confess the Apostles' Creed and the Nicene Creed. Both hold the Trinity. Both hold that Jesus is fully God and fully man, born of a virgin, crucified under Pontius Pilate, bodily raised on the third day, and coming again. Both hold that salvation is possible only through Christ, and that it begins with God's grace rather than human effort.","That is not a small overlap. That is the core of Christianity, confessed in the same words, in the same creeds, by both."],"thisWeek":"read the Apostles' Creed once. It is about a hundred words. Ask yourself which line in it you would want a family member to deny.","disagree":null},{"title":"Where the disagreement is actually real.","ref":"Galatians 2:16","body":["Now the honest part, because glossing over this would be its own kind of lie.","The Reformation was not a misunderstanding. The central dispute is over justification: whether a person is declared righteous by faith alone, or whether grace works through faith *and* the works it produces, so that the two cannot be separated in the way Protestants separate them. There are also real differences over the authority of Scripture alongside church tradition, the role of Mary, and prayers for the dead.","These are genuine. Serious people have divided over them for five hundred years and I am not dissolving them in a sentence.","**One fact worth knowing, because almost nobody does:** in 1999 the Catholic Church and the Lutheran World Federation signed a Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification, stating a shared understanding of justification by grace through faith, while still noting remaining differences. Not everyone on either side accepts it as settling things. But the two bodies that split over this went and wrote down how much they now agree, and that is worth knowing before you decide the gap is total."],"thisWeek":"if you argue about this with someone, argue about justification, the actual issue, rather than about statues, which is not it.","disagree":null},{"title":"Nobody gets in on membership.","ref":"Matthew 7:21–23","body":["This is the verse that should frighten everyone equally, and it is usually aimed only outward.","The people Jesus turns away here are not pagans. They are religious professionals with an impressive ministry CV. And the thing He says is not \\"you believed the wrong doctrine\\"; it is *I never knew you.* The failure is relational.","So the warning lands on the Catholic who has gone to Mass for sixty years without ever trusting Christ. And it lands just as hard on the Baptist who can define justification precisely and has never spoken to God about anything."],"thisWeek":"apply this verse to yourself before you apply it to a relative. That is the only order in which it is safe to read.","disagree":null},{"title":"What to do about the person you are actually asking about.","ref":"1 Corinthians 4:5","body":["You cannot see someone's heart. Neither can the person who told you your grandmother is in hell.","Paul says the sorting is Christ's, at a time that has not arrived, using evidence nobody else has access to. Holding a verdict you are not equipped to reach is not conviction; it is overreach, and it will cost you the relationship you still have.","Here is what you can actually do. Ask your Catholic family member what they are trusting in. Not as a trap, as a real question. You will often find they answer with Christ, in their own words, and the conversation you were dreading turns into the best one you have had with them."],"thisWeek":"ask one person in your family that question. Then listen to the whole answer without correcting any of it.","disagree":null}]},{"id":"ep44","title":"How to Start Your Morning With God","category":"daily","disputed":false,"crisis":false,"short":["God before the phone. Charge it in another room.","Attach it to the kettle. Same chair, every day.","One psalm. Not a plan.","Name the one thing you dread, out loud.","Take one line with you."],"intro":["You do not need an hour. You do not need to become a morning person. Five things, about ten minutes, and the difference is not that you feel holier; it is that the day stops starting without you.","Here is what you are actually up against, and it is not laziness.","It is that your morning is not neutral territory. It belongs to whoever gets there first. You reach for the phone before your feet hit the floor, and within ninety seconds you have absorbed four opinions, two pieces of bad news and somebody else's holiday. Your nervous system is awake and braced before you have had a single thought of your own.","Then at some point you think *I should pray*, and it feels like trying to start a quiet conversation in a room where the television is already on.","Nothing in this lesson works until that one thing changes.","Five tips. Each one is small, each is specific, and the first one is the only one that is hard. This is for you, and it is for whoever you keep meaning to send a verse to at eleven at night."],"steps":[{"title":"God before the phone.","ref":"Psalm 5:3","body":["Twice in one verse: *in the morning*, then *at daybreak*. David is not being poetic about timing. He is describing a habit of going first.","The practical version is unglamorous. Charge the phone across the room, or outside the bedroom. That is the whole technique, and it does more than any amount of resolve, because resolve at 6 a.m. is not a thing most people have.","And notice the last words: *wait in expectation*. He is not firing off requests and running. He assumes an answer is coming and he stays for a moment to see."],"thisWeek":"charge your phone outside the bedroom for seven nights. Not forever. Seven nights. Notice what your first ten minutes turn into.","disagree":null},{"title":"Attach it to something you already do.","ref":"Mark 1:35","body":["Look at how specific that is. A time. A departure. A place.","Jesus did not pray when He felt moved to. He had a slot and a location, and when a crowd of people who genuinely needed Him came looking, He had already gone.","You do not need a hillside. You need the same chair and an existing trigger: the kettle, the coffee, the first ten minutes before anyone else is up. Attach the new habit to something your body already does automatically and you stop relying on memory."],"thisWeek":"pick the trigger out loud. \\"While the coffee brews, I read.\\" One sentence, decided once, so you are not deciding it again every morning in the dark.","disagree":null},{"title":"Read one psalm, not a plan.","ref":"Lamentations 3:22–23","body":["New *every morning*. Not stockpiled. Not drawn down by yesterday. Whatever you did last night, the supply at 7 a.m. is full.","That is why morning reading should be short and warm rather than ambitious. The most common way people kill this habit is a year-long plan begun in January that has them in Leviticus by February, bored, behind and quietly ashamed.","One psalm. That is it. There are a hundred and fifty of them and they are written for exactly this: the full range, including the furious ones."],"thisWeek":"start at Psalm 1 and read one a day in order. When one lands, stay on it a second day instead of moving on. Nobody is marking this.","disagree":null},{"title":"Hand over the one thing you are dreading. Out loud.","ref":"Psalm 143:8","body":["There is usually one thing. The conversation you have to have. The appointment. The email you have not opened. It is sitting on your chest before you are properly awake.","Name that one thing, out loud, in a sentence. \\"This is the thing I am dreading today. I am handing it to you. Show me the way.\\"","Out loud matters, and not for God's benefit. A worry that stays inside your head keeps circling. Said out loud it becomes a thing you have put somewhere. David asks, *teach me the way I should walk*. The prayer is for the next step, not for the whole map."],"thisWeek":"one sentence, spoken, naming the actual thing. Not \\"bless my day.\\"","disagree":null},{"title":"Take one line with you.","ref":"Psalm 119:11","body":["Here is what makes a morning habit count by three in the afternoon: you leave with something.","Pick one line from what you read (not the whole chapter, one line) and carry it. Say it in the car. Put it on a sticky note on the monitor. The psalmist's word is *hidden*: stored somewhere internal, so it is available when you are nowhere near a Bible and something is going wrong.","This is also the step that makes the other four stop feeling like a box ticked. A morning routine that ends when you stand up is a ritual. One that hands you a sentence for 3 p.m. is a supply line."],"thisWeek":"one line a day, written on something physical you will see at work. Paper, not a note app. The app is the thing you were avoiding.","disagree":null}]},{"id":"ep45","title":"Is Astrology a Sin?","category":"culture","disputed":false,"crisis":false,"short":["The Bible names it (\\"interprets omens\\") and is not vague.","The issue is the *source* you go to, not that stars are spooky.","The sky's job is worship and time, not prediction.","The Magi were met inside it, and dropped it when they found Him.","You are not asked to care less about the future. You are offered someone who sees it."],"intro":["You check your horoscope. Maybe not seriously. Maybe it is just there in the app, and you read it the way you read the weather. And some part of you has wondered whether it counts as something you should stop.","The Bible does speak to this directly. More directly than most things people ask me about.","There are two answers you will get, and both leave you worse off.","The first: it is harmless fun, do not be so uptight. Which does not deal with why you were uneasy enough to search it.","The second: you have opened a door and invited demons into your home. Which is said with enormous confidence and usually no verse, and sends people into a fear that is not from God either.","The actual biblical case is more interesting than both, and once you see what it is about, the question answers itself without anyone having to frighten you.","Five things. Including the strongest argument *against* my position, which is a story you already know and have probably heard used to defend astrology."],"steps":[{"title":"The Bible names it, and is not vague.","ref":"Deuteronomy 18:10–12","body":["That is not a borderline passage. *Interprets omens* is the phrase that covers reading meaning out of signs, and reading the future off the position of the planets is precisely that.","Before you brace: notice what the passage is actually governing. These are listed as the practices of the nations Israel was moving among, and the instruction is about where Israel goes for guidance instead. The next verses say God will send them a prophet and they are to listen to him.","So the issue is not that the subject is spooky. It is a question of *source*."],"thisWeek":"notice whether you check the horoscope before or after you pray. The order tells you what it actually functions as.","disagree":null},{"title":"The problem is where you are going for the answer.","ref":"Isaiah 47:13–14","body":["This is the clearest passage on it, and the tone is worth hearing. God is not mainly angry. He is pointing out that it does not work.","*You are wearied by your many counselors.* That is a diagnosis a lot of people recognise: the exhausting business of checking, and interpreting, and checking again, and never actually being steadier.","Then the challenge: let them save you from what is coming. He is not saying the stars are powerful rivals. He is saying they cannot do the one thing you are going to them for."],"thisWeek":"if you use astrology, ask honestly what you go to it *for*. Almost always the answer is reassurance about something specific. Name that thing. It is the real subject.","disagree":null},{"title":"The stars have a job, and this is not it.","ref":"Psalm 19:1","body":["The Bible is not anti-star. It is wildly enthusiastic about them.","But look at what they are doing here. They *declare* and *proclaim*: they point at the one who made them. They are a signpost, and astrology is reading the signpost instead of the destination.","God also uses them for timekeeping: Genesis 1:14 gives the lights the job of marking seasons, days and years. A calendar, which is the thing astronomy grew out of. The Bible gives the sky two jobs, worship and time, and personal prediction is in neither."],"thisWeek":"go outside once this week and look up for two minutes without your phone. That is what the psalm is describing and almost nobody does it.","disagree":null},{"title":"The strongest objection: the wise men.","ref":"Matthew 2:1–2","body":["Here is the argument, and it is a fair one: star-watchers followed a star and found Jesus, and God evidently used it. So how can reading the sky be forbidden?","Taking it seriously: three things.","The Magi were most likely star-watching scholars from Persia or Babylon, and the text does not condemn them. God met them inside the thing they already did and drew them out of it. That is a real pattern in scripture and it is genuinely gracious.","But notice what the star did. It did not tell them about their week. It did not predict their temperament or their romantic prospects. It pointed to a person, once, and the moment they arrived they stopped looking up and fell down and worshipped.","**And the honest part:** this is a story about what God did, not an instruction about what we should do. The same Bible that records the Magi prohibits the practice elsewhere. If you want to follow their example, the example is that they dropped it the moment they found Him."],"thisWeek":"read Matthew 2:1–12 and watch how quickly the star stops being the subject.","disagree":null},{"title":"What to actually do with the app.","ref":"Proverbs 3:5–6","body":["So: delete it, or not?","Here is the better question. The verse contrasts trusting God with leaning on *your own understanding*: the whole business of trying to get out in front of your life and secure it yourself. Astrology is one of the more elegant forms of that, and the reason it is so sticky is that it offers the one thing anxiety wants: a preview.","You are not being asked to be less interested in your future. You are being offered a better arrangement: someone who already sees it, who you can actually speak to, and who promises to direct the path rather than describe it to you in advance.","So, practically: yes, take it off your phone. Not because a verse will zap you, but because you will keep going back to a thing that is one tap away. And when you catch yourself wanting to know what is coming, say so to God in those exact words. That is a prayer, and it is the one astrology was standing in for."],"thisWeek":"delete the app. Then, the first time you reach for it out of habit, say out loud, \\"I want to know what's coming and I don't.\\" Hand that sentence over. That is the whole exchange.","disagree":null}]},{"id":"ep46","title":"What Is Purgatory?","category":"churches","disputed":true,"crisis":false,"short":["Everyone in purgatory is already saved. It is not a third destination.","Its main text is 1 Corinthians 3: the fire that tests the work.","Protestants answer with Hebrews 10:14: made perfect forever.","The two sides have different Bibles. Name it first, not last.","Both agree: nobody enters unchanged, and the change is God's."],"intro":["Most people arguing about purgatory are arguing about something that is not the doctrine. Including, usually, the people most confident about it.","So here is what the Catholic Church actually teaches, the verses it rests on, why Protestants reject it, and the one thing both sides agree on completely.","The common Protestant picture is this: purgatory is a waiting room for people who were not quite good enough for heaven, where you suffer off the balance until you qualify.","If that were the teaching, it would be a direct attack on the cross and every Protestant objection would be obvious.","But that is not the teaching, and arguing against it is arguing with nobody. Meanwhile a real disagreement, a serious one, goes undiscussed, and families split over a cartoon.","Five things. I am Protestant and I will tell you where I land, but I am going to give the Catholic position in its own terms first, because you cannot disagree well with something you have not understood."],"steps":[{"title":"What it actually claims.","ref":"Hebrews 12:14","body":["Here is the teaching as the Catechism gives it. Purgatory is a final purification for those who **die in God's grace and friendship** but are still imperfectly purified. And the key line, which almost nobody on my side mentions: everyone in purgatory is **already saved**. Their salvation is certain. It is not a third destination and it is not a second chance.","So it is not about qualifying. It is about being cleaned up on the way in.","And you can see the instinct it comes from: it is that verse. Without holiness no one will see the Lord. Everyone who has ever died knows they were not finished. The question purgatory tries to answer is a real one: what happens to the gap?"],"thisWeek":"notice that you have an answer to that question too, even if you have never said it out loud.","disagree":null},{"title":"The texts it rests on.","ref":"1 Corinthians 3:13–15","body":["This is the main New Testament passage, and you can see why it is used. A fire. Something burned away. A person who still ends up saved, but as one escaping through flames.","The other major text is 2 Maccabees 12, which describes prayer and offerings for the dead, and that is where the conversation usually stops dead, because 2 Maccabees is in Catholic Bibles and not in Protestant ones. We will come back to that.","**The Protestant reading of 1 Corinthians 3, fairly stated:** Paul is talking about the testing of a person's *work*, what they built, not the purifying of the person. The fire burns the building, not the builder. That is, I think, the better reading of the passage. But I understand how someone arrives at the other one."],"thisWeek":"read 1 Corinthians 3:10–15 whole and decide for yourself what is in the fire. That is the actual question.","disagree":null},{"title":"Why Protestants reject it.","ref":"Hebrews 10:14","body":["This is the heart of the objection, and it is not about whether purification is needed. It is about whether it is finished.","*Made perfect for all time*: already done, by a single offering. (Fair note: the same verse calls them *those who are being sanctified*, which is still going on.) The Reformers' argument was that if the cross made us perfect forever, adding a further process of purification after death implies the cross left something outstanding.","Catholics answer that purgatory is not additional payment but the *application* of what Christ already won: not topping up the price, but the soul being made able to bear the presence it is already guaranteed. That is a real answer and it is not a dodge.","Where I land: Hebrews 10:14 is the stronger verse, and I do not think the New Testament describes purgatory. But notice that this is a disagreement about *timing and mechanism*, not about whether Christ's work saves. Both sides say it does."],"thisWeek":"say that last sentence back to yourself before the next time this comes up at a family table.","disagree":null},{"title":"Why the two sides have different Bibles.","ref":"2 Timothy 3:16","body":["Here is the thing that makes this argument unwinnable if nobody names it.","Catholic Bibles contain seven books Protestant Bibles do not, including 2 Maccabees. Catholics hold them as Scripture; the Church formally confirmed the list at Trent in the sixteenth century, drawing on much older usage. Protestants follow the shorter Hebrew canon and call those books useful but not Scripture.","So when a Catholic cites 2 Maccabees on prayer for the dead, they are citing their Bible. And when a Protestant says \\"that is not in the Bible,\\" they are telling the truth about theirs. Both are being honest. They are not working from the same book, and until that is on the table the argument just goes in circles with each side thinking the other is dodging."],"thisWeek":"if you argue this one, open with the canon question rather than ending with it. It changes the entire conversation.","disagree":null},{"title":"What both sides agree on completely.","ref":"1 John 3:2","body":["Nobody enters God's presence unchanged. On that there is no dispute at all.","Every Christian tradition holds that something happens between the person you are now and the person who will stand there, and that the change is God's work, not yours. Protestants call it glorification and locate it at Christ's appearing. Catholics include a purification for those who need it. Both are describing God finishing something.","And read what John says the mechanism is: *we will be like Him, for we will see Him as He is.* Not scrubbed by a process. Changed by the sight of Him."],"thisWeek":"if someone you love died Catholic and you have been told their church's teaching means they are not safe, that is not what their church teaches, and it is not what yours does either. Put it down.","disagree":null}]},{"id":"ep47","title":"Is Cremation a Sin?","category":"death","disputed":false,"crisis":false,"short":["There is no prohibition anywhere in scripture.","Burial was the custom and it pictures the resurrection.","The sea gives up its dead. Condition is not a factor.","Honour the body either way: give people a place.","Be fully convinced in your own mind, and decide on the real factors."],"intro":["Short answer first, because some of you are deciding this week and do not need a slow build: no, the Bible nowhere forbids cremation, and nothing about it puts a person outside the resurrection.","Now the long answer, because there is more here than a yes or no, and the reason people ask is usually not the reason they say.","Here is the fear underneath, and almost nobody says it out loud.","It is not really \\"is it a sin.\\" It is: *if the body is ashes, is there anything left for God to raise?* Somebody has heard a preacher say cremation denies the resurrection, and now they are lying awake about a decision they already made for their mother three years ago.","That fear deserves a direct answer rather than a careful one, and the Bible gives it a direct answer. We will get there in step three, and if you only read one part of this, read that one.","Five things: what the Bible does and does not say, why burial was the custom, the verse that settles the resurrection fear, why it still matters how you treat a body, and how to actually decide."],"steps":[{"title":"There is no prohibition.","ref":"Genesis 3:19","body":["This is the Bible's own description of what happens to a body, and it is the same destination either way. Dust. Cremation is the same process at a different speed.","Search the law and you will find detailed instruction on a great many things (diet, cloth, mildew, debt, how to build a roof) and not one line forbidding the burning of a body after death. If this were the spiritual catastrophe some teaching makes of it, the silence would be very strange.","Where burning appears negatively in scripture it is as a *judgement* (Achan in Joshua 7), which is about what was done to someone as punishment, not about a family's choice at a funeral. Those are different acts."],"thisWeek":"if someone tells you it is forbidden, ask which verse. Kindly. There isn't one, and the asking is not rude.","disagree":null},{"title":"Burial was the custom, and it meant something.","ref":"John 19:40","body":["Let me not overstate my case. The consistent pattern in scripture is burial. Abraham buys a field to bury Sarah. Joseph's bones are carried out of Egypt. Jesus Himself was buried, and that matters.","And there is a reason beyond custom. Paul's picture of resurrection is a seed sown in the ground and coming up as something greater. Burial acts that picture out. It says this person is planted, not disposed of.","So burial is not merely traditional. It preaches something. If you can bury and want to, that is a good instinct and you do not need talking out of it."],"thisWeek":"if you are choosing, ask what each option *says* to the people standing there. That is a legitimate thing to weigh.","disagree":null},{"title":"Your resurrection does not depend on your body's condition.","ref":"Revelation 20:13","body":["Here is the answer to the fear.","The sea gives up its dead. Think about what that means. Those bodies are not intact somewhere waiting. They were lost, dispersed, gone in every ordinary sense, centuries of them, and the text says they are given up all the same.","Add the Christians who were burned at the stake for refusing to deny Christ. Nobody has ever suggested the manner of their death put them beyond resurrection. The idea collapses the moment you say it next to a martyr.","God is not working from salvaged material. The One who made a body out of dust in the first place is not short of raw material, and the resurrection has never been a matter of reassembly."],"thisWeek":"if you have been carrying guilt about a decision you made for someone you loved, that is the verse to put it down on. You did not cost them anything.","disagree":null},{"title":"It still matters how you treat the body.","ref":"1 Corinthians 6:19–20","body":["Permitted is not the same as unimportant.","The Christian view of the body is high, higher than the culture's. Not a shell the real you escapes, but something God made, lives in, and intends to raise. That is why Christians have always buried their dead with care instead of simply disposing of them, and why the early church's funerals looked so strange to Rome.","So the question is not only *may I*, but *does what we do honour the person and point to the resurrection*. A cremation can do that completely: with a service, a reading, a place, a gathering, somewhere for people to come back to. It is when the body is treated as an admin problem that something has gone wrong, and that can happen with a burial too."],"thisWeek":"if you are planning one, give people a place. Ashes with a named spot do more good for a grieving family than a box on a shelf.","disagree":null},{"title":"How to actually decide.","ref":"Romans 14:5","body":["This is the chapter Paul writes for exactly this kind of question: matters real Christians decide differently without either being in sin.","And his instruction is not \\"work out the right answer.\\" It is *be fully convinced in your own mind*, and then (read the rest of Romans 14) do not despise the person who chose otherwise.","So decide on the real factors. Cost, and there is no shame in that; most families are choosing partly on cost and pretending otherwise. What the family needs in order to grieve. Whether there is a place people can return to. What the person themselves wanted.","What should not be in the list is fear that God will be unable to find them."],"thisWeek":"if it is your own funeral you are thinking about, tell someone what you want, in writing. The kindest thing you can do for your family is remove the decision from them on the worst week of their lives.","disagree":null}]},{"id":"ep48","title":"The Beast Isn't Who You Think. Here's What Revelation Says.","category":"endtimes","disputed":true,"crisis":false,"short":["Seven heads, ten horns: the text points at empire before any individual.","\\"Calculate it\\" and the 616 variant both point back to the first century. Nero fits best.","Reading one: it already happened. Rome and the imperial cult.","Reading two: it is still coming. Strong on verse 17, weak on its own track record.","Reading three: it is the pattern. And the chapter's own last word is *endurance*."],"intro":["If you have been told who the beast is, you have been told wrong at least once. Probably more.","I am going to show you what Revelation 13 actually describes, the three readings serious Christians hold, and (the part almost nobody gets to) what the chapter was written to *do*. Because it was not written to be solved.","Here is the pattern, and once you see it you cannot unsee it.","Every generation has identified the beast with total confidence. Nero. The Pope. Napoleon. The Kaiser. Hitler. Mussolini. Stalin. Henry Kissinger. Gorbachev, because of the birthmark. Every one of those was preached from pulpits by people who were certain.","They cannot all have been right. And the lesson is not that people keep getting close; it is that the *method* is wrong. Take whoever frightens you most this decade, work backwards to the text, and the text will seem to cooperate. It always does.","So we are going to go the other way round. Text first.","Five things: what the chapter actually says, the number and the best candidate for it, the three readings, and what it is for. I will tell you where the evidence genuinely runs out rather than selling you a name."],"steps":[{"title":"What the text actually describes.","ref":"Revelation 13:1","body":["Read that as John wrote it, not as a film poster.","Seven heads. Ten horns. Ten crowns. Blasphemous names written on it. This is not a description of a man you could pass in the street; it is a composite, and it is built out of Daniel 7, where four beasts represent four kingdoms. John's beast has the features of all of them at once.","So the first thing the text does is point at **empire**, not at an individual. Heads and horns in this literature are rulers and kingdoms. The imagery is political before it is personal.","That does not settle whether a person is also in view. It does mean anyone who starts with \\"which man is it\\" has skipped the first verse."],"thisWeek":"read Revelation 13 straight through once, slowly. Eighteen verses. Notice how much of it is about a system and how little is about a face.","disagree":null},{"title":"The number, and the strongest candidate.","ref":"Revelation 13:18","body":["Two things in that verse get skipped.","First, John expects his readers to be able to work it out. *Let the one who has insight calculate*. He is not writing a riddle for the twenty-first century, he is writing to seven churches in Asia Minor who he assumes can do the sum.","Second: *it is the number of a man.* So a person is in view somewhere.","In Hebrew and Greek, letters double as numerals, and adding a name's letters was a known game. The candidate most scholars find strongest: **Nero Caesar**, written in Hebrew letters, totals 666. And there is a genuinely striking piece of evidence: some early manuscripts of Revelation read **616** instead of 666, and the Latin spelling of Nero's name drops a letter and totals exactly 616. A variant that tracks a spelling change is hard to explain by accident.","**The honest limit:** gematria can be made to fit many names, which is exactly how every generation's candidate got certified. Nero is the best fit for the original readers. It is not a proof."],"thisWeek":"notice that the two strongest clues, \\"calculate it\\" and the 616 variant, both point back at the first century, not forward at ours.","disagree":null},{"title":"Reading one: it already happened.","ref":"Revelation 13:7","body":["This is the preterist reading, and it is held by a great many conservative scholars.","On this view the beast is Rome, and particularly the imperial cult. Verse 7 describes exactly what was happening: a state waging war on Christians, with authority over every people in the known world. Nero burned believers. The empire demanded the confession *Caesar is Lord*, which is precisely the blasphemous name on the heads in verse 1.","Its strength is that it takes seriously who John was writing *to*. People being killed that year needed a word about that year.","**Its weak point:** chapter 13 sits inside a book that ends with the final judgement and a new creation, and those plainly have not happened. The reading has to account for that."],"thisWeek":null,"disagree":null},{"title":"Reading two: it is still coming.","ref":"Revelation 13:16–17","body":["This is the futurist reading, the most common in popular teaching, and it has a real argument.","A single authority controlling whether every person on earth can buy or sell is more conceivable now than at any point in history. The text describes a total economic system, and Rome never had one.","**Its weak point, stated plainly:** this is the reading that produces the parade of wrong names in the intro. Every generation that held it has identified a contemporary figure and been wrong. That does not make it false, but it should make anyone holding it slow to name anybody.","And note what the text itself says: a mark on the hand or forehead, and the word microchip is not in the Bible."],"thisWeek":null,"disagree":null},{"title":"What the chapter is actually for.","ref":"Revelation 13:10","body":["Here is the third reading and the payoff of the whole lesson.","The idealist reading says the beast is the recurring pattern of any power that demands the worship owed to God: Rome then, other regimes since, and a final form at the end. On this view you are not waiting to identify the beast; you are learning to recognise the shape.","And look at the line John ends the section with. Not *so work out who it is*. Not *so watch the news*. **Here is a call for the perseverance and faith of the saints.**","That is the purpose of the chapter, stated inside the chapter. It was written to people who were losing, to tell them that the power crushing them was on a leash and had a limit, and that their job was to hold on.","Which means a person who finishes Revelation 13 frightened and scanning headlines has had the opposite experience from the one it was written to produce. It is a chapter for steadiness."],"thisWeek":"read verse 10 again and ask what in your life it is actually asking of you. For almost nobody is the answer \\"identify a politician.\\"","disagree":null}]}]`);export{e as n,t};